      'A clear talk connects purpose, audience and evidence. In an invented three-minute council presentation, a speaker proposes a limited library trial. The opening states the request, the middle explains relevant demand and cost questions, and the ending states the decision sought. Signposting such as First, the demand; next, the cost helps listeners follow. If a chart is used, explain its measure, axes and takeaway rather than merely reading labels. Spoken citation identifies a relevant source in understandable form. When asked for unmeasured learning benefits, acknowledge the gap rather than inventing a result. Accessibility can include a readable visual and a written summary.',
