      'A rigorous reader separates kinds of claims. Invented argument: Evening opening increased recorded visits. Therefore it caused learning gains, and every school should adopt it. The observed variable is visits. The causal learning claim needs an appropriate outcome and a design addressing competing explanations. The normative recommendation also assumes benefits justify costs across different contexts. Even if one local benefit were established, universal adoption would need additional evidence and value reasoning. Charitable reconstruction states the strongest accurate version of the argument before critique; it does not add premises the author never accepts. Evaluate what is supported, what is assumed and what would resolve the uncertainty.',
      'Reconstruct the argument. 1. Identify the visit observation. 2. Separate causal learning claim. 3. Separate universal recommendation. 4. Name missing learning and feasibility support. 5. Offer a narrower conclusion: investigate whether a local trial is useful.',
      'Give a counterexample to If visits rose, learning must have risen that is consistent with the measured variable.',
