      'Invented source for practice: Orin (2024), Access Trial Notes, page 7, states: Attendance increased during four evening sessions; learning outcomes were not assessed. A quotation must reproduce the selected wording accurately and mark it as borrowed. A paraphrase changes wording and structure while preserving both the increased attendance and the learning limit. Citation gives attribution and enough information to locate evidence according to the assigned style. A bibliographic entry must match the source actually used. If you only encountered an original claim quoted in another work, follow the course requirements for secondary citation and do not pretend to have read the original. Orin and this document are invented, not retrievable research.',
