      ],
      'An argumentative essay develops a contestable thesis through reasons, evidence and warrants. Invented proposal: A school should test evening library access for one term because documented requests suggest unmet local demand. The warrant connects requests with potential use, but assumes requesting pupils can attend and staffing is feasible. Address those assumptions with a trial design and records rather than claiming every school benefits. A counterargument about staff workload deserves a real response. Conceding that workload must be measured strengthens a limited proposal; it does not require abandoning the thesis. In real writing, replace all illustrative claims with authentic documented evidence.',
